= Scientific model — Technical =

== Model–target relation ==
Let model <code>M</code> be used to represent target <code>T</code> for purpose <code>P</code>.

Representation is not exhausted by structural similarity.

Scientific representation can depend on:
* interpretation;
* intended use;
* idealization;
* inferential affordances;
* user conventions;
* mathematical mapping;
* similarity in selected respects.

A model can represent T well for P while being poor for another purpose.

== Mathematical models ==
A dynamical model may take the form:

<pre>
dx/dt = f(x, θ, u, t)
</pre>

where:
* <code>x</code> = state;
* <code>θ</code> = parameters;
* <code>u</code> = inputs/controls.

Inference may estimate θ from data, while validation tests whether model behavior is adequate for the target domain.

== Statistical models ==
A statistical model specifies a family of probability distributions:

<pre>
{ P_θ : θ ∈ Θ }
</pre>

Model fitting selects/estimates θ or compares model families.

Good fit is not enough: misspecification, overfitting, dependence, and distribution shift can undermine inference.

== Computational models ==
A computational model is implemented through algorithms and software.

Important distinctions:

<pre>
conceptual/scientific model
≠ mathematical formulation
≠ numerical method
≠ program implementation
≠ simulation output
</pre>

Each layer introduces different possible errors.

== Verification, calibration, validation ==
'''Verification''' — are equations/algorithms implemented/solved correctly?

'''Calibration''' — which parameter values align model outputs with calibration data?

'''Validation''' — is the model adequate for the intended real-world use?

A model can pass one and fail another.

== Structural discrepancy ==
Even with optimal parameters, a model can systematically differ from the target because its structure is incomplete.

A useful inferential formulation separates:

<pre>
observed data
= model prediction
+ model discrepancy
+ measurement error
</pre>

Identifiability between these components can be difficult.

== Sensitivity ==
Local or global sensitivity analysis studies:

<pre>
∂ output / ∂ parameter
</pre>

or more general changes in output distributions under parameter/input variation.

Sensitivity is not the same as uncertainty: a parameter can be highly influential but known precisely, or uncertain but have little effect.

== Validation domain ==
Validation should specify a domain:

<pre>
D = {inputs, states, environments, scales, uses}
</pre>

Evidence inside D does not automatically justify extrapolation outside D.

